= Solution
{parent=a}

Use the $+---$ <metric signature>, with the time component of <four-momentum> equal to $E/c$. The incident particle has <energy> $\gamma mc^2$ and <momentum> of magnitude $mc\sqrt{\gamma^2-1}$. Adding the stationary target's <four-momentum> gives total laboratory quantities
$$
E_{\rm tot}=(\gamma+2)mc^2,\qquad |\mathbf p_{\rm tot}|=mc\sqrt{\gamma^2-1}.
$$
The <centre-of-momentum energy> $W$ follows from the invariant <Minkowski norm> of the total <four-momentum>:
$$
W^2=E_{\rm tot}^2-c^2|\mathbf p_{\rm tot}|^2
=m^2c^4\big[(\gamma+2)^2-(\gamma^2-1)\big]
=(4\gamma+5)m^2c^4.
$$
At the <relativistic collision mass threshold>, the two final particles have zero relative <velocity> in their <centre-of-momentum frame>. Their total <energy> there is therefore $2\alpha mc^2$. <Four-momentum conservation> requires $W\geq2\alpha mc^2$, so
$$
\boxed{\gamma_{\min}=\alpha^2-\frac54.}
$$
The condition $\alpha>3/2$ ensures $\gamma_{\min}>1$. This is the **minimum required** <Lorentz factor>; larger values allow final <kinetic energy> in the <centre-of-momentum frame>. Equivalently, the incident laboratory <kinetic energy> at threshold is $(\alpha^2-9/4)mc^2$.

= b
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

The required invariant is obtained by <tensor contraction>, rather than by an ordinary sum of squares of the entries of the <electromagnetic field tensor>. With $\eta_{\mu\nu}=\operatorname{diag}(1,-1,-1,-1)$, raising both indices gives
$$
F^{0i}=-F_{0i},\qquad F^{ij}=F_{ij}\quad(i,j=1,2,3).
$$
The <electromagnetic field tensor> is an <antisymmetric second-rank tensor>, so every independent off-diagonal entry occurs twice in its contraction. The three electric contributions each acquire a minus sign, whereas the magnetic contributions retain their sign. Consequently
$$
F_{\mu\nu}F^{\mu\nu}
=2\left[(B^1)^2+(B^2)^2+(B^3)^2
-\frac{(E^1)^2+(E^2)^2+(E^3)^2}{c^2}\right].
$$
All indices are contracted, so this quantity is a <Lorentz scalar>. Since the <speed of light> is invariant,
$$
\boxed{c^2|\mathbf B|^2-|\mathbf E|^2=\frac{c^2}{2}F_{\mu\nu}F^{\mu\nu}}
$$
is <Lorentz invariant>. Both terms have the units of squared <electric field>, $\mathrm{V^2\,m^{-2}}$ in SI units.

= c
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

The <cosmological principle> gives <spatial homogeneity> and <cosmological isotropy> for the large-scale spatial slices. About any chosen <comoving observer>, <spherical coordinates> therefore separate a radial direction from the angular directions. In particular,
$$
d\Omega^2=d\theta^2+\sin^2\theta\,d\phi^2
$$
is the <metric tensor> on the unit <sphere>. Its appearance expresses <cosmological isotropy>, rather than a privileged centre of the universe. The factor $(1-Kr^2)^{-1}$ permits the radial geometry to have nonzero <spatial curvature of an FLRW universe>.

The <scale factor> $a(t)$ changes every spatial separation between fixed <comoving coordinates> by the same multiplicative factor. For example, the radial <proper distance in cosmology> between fixed radial labels at a fixed <cosmic time> is
$$
D(t)=a(t)\int_{r_1}^{r_2}\frac{dr}{\sqrt{1-Kr^2}}.
$$
Expansion or contraction through this single function preserves <spatial homogeneity> and <cosmological isotropy>; direction-dependent scale factors would generally spoil the latter. The <Friedmann-Lemaître-Robertson-Walker metric> specifies this possible evolution geometrically; a dynamical theory is needed to determine $a(t)$.

The constant $K$ fixes the sign and normalization of the <spatial curvature of an FLRW universe>. The spatial <scalar curvature> is ${}^{(3)}\!\mathcal R=6K/a^2(t)$. With dimensionless $r$ and a suitably normalized <scale factor>, the familiar choices are $K=+1,0,-1$ for spherical, flat, and hyperbolic spatial geometry. A rescaling of $r$ changes the normalization of $K$ and $a$ together; its sign has invariant geometric significance. The local <metric tensor> alone does not fix global <topology>.

The coordinates share a **cosmological, comoving construction**: $r,\theta,\phi$ label the worldlines of the smooth cosmic fluid, and $t$ is the common <cosmic time> measured as <proper time> along those worldlines. Observers following that fluid keep their spatial labels while the <scale factor> changes their physical separations. The coordinate $t$ itself increases; it is not another constant spatial label. Thus the question's final phrase is understood as asking for their common adaptation to the cosmological fluid, rather than suggesting that all four coordinates are time independent or have the same units.

