= B2
{c}
{parent=Section B}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=B2}

The two <postulates of special relativity> are: **all nongravitational laws have the same form in every <inertial frame>**, and **light in vacuum has the same finite <speed of light> $c$ in every <inertial frame>, independently of the motion of its source**. The first is <Einstein's principle of relativity>; the second supplies an experimentally identified invariant speed.

For the stated standard configuration, the <Lorentz transformation> is
$$
x'=\gamma_v(x-vt),\qquad t'=\gamma_v\left(t-\frac{vx}{c^2}\right),\qquad y'=y,\qquad z'=z,
\qquad \gamma_v=\frac1{\sqrt{1-v^2/c^2}}.
$$
Take <differentials> along the particle's <worldline>, rather than comparing coordinate displacements at equal times in the two frames. Writing $u_i=dx^i/dt$, one obtains
$$
dt'=\gamma_v\left(1-\frac{vu_x}{c^2}\right)dt,\qquad
dx'=\gamma_v(u_x-v)dt,\qquad dy'=u_y\,dt,\qquad dz'=u_z\,dt.
$$
Dividing by the transformed time interval proves the <velocity-addition formula>:
$$
\boxed{u'_x=\frac{u_x-v}{1-vu_x/c^2},\quad
u'_y=\frac{u_y}{\gamma_v(1-vu_x/c^2)},\quad
u'_z=\frac{u_z}{\gamma_v(1-vu_x/c^2)}.}
$$
For a massive particle, $|\mathbf u|<c$ and $|v|<c$, so the denominator $1-vu_x/c^2$ is positive.

= a
{parent=B2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

Let $\mathbf a=d\mathbf u/dt$ denote coordinate <acceleration> and use $A^\mu$ for <four-acceleration>, to distinguish these two objects. Applying the <chain rule> to the <Lorentz factor> gives
$$
\frac{d\gamma_u}{dt}
=-\frac12\left(1-\frac{\mathbf u\cdot\mathbf u}{c^2}\right)^{-3/2}
\left(-\frac{2\mathbf u\cdot\mathbf a}{c^2}\right)
=\boxed{\frac{\gamma_u^3\mathbf u\cdot\mathbf a}{c^2}}.
$$
Since $dt/d\tau=\gamma_u$, differentiation of the <four-velocity> $U^\mu=\gamma_u(c,\mathbf u)$ with respect to <proper time> yields
$$
A^\mu=\gamma_u\frac{d}{dt}\big[\gamma_u(c,\mathbf u)\big]
=\boxed{\left(\frac{\gamma_u^4\mathbf u\cdot\mathbf a}{c},\
\gamma_u^2\mathbf a+\frac{\gamma_u^4(\mathbf u\cdot\mathbf a)\mathbf u}{c^2}\right)}.
$$
This is also $\gamma_u^2[\gamma_u^2(\mathbf u\cdot\mathbf a)/c,\ \mathbf a+\gamma_u^2(\mathbf u\cdot\mathbf a)\mathbf u/c^2]$, as required.

The invariant <Minkowski norm> of the <four-velocity> is $U^\mu U_\mu=c^2$. Differentiating this identity gives $U^\mu A_\mu=0$. In the instantaneous <rest frame>, $U^\mu=(c,\mathbf0)$, so $A^0=0$ and the spatial components equal the physically experienced <proper acceleration> $\boldsymbol\alpha$. Therefore $\alpha^2=-A^\mu A_\mu$ in the $+---$ <metric signature>.

For a direct calculation, put $q=\mathbf u\cdot\mathbf a$ and expand the temporal and spatial squares:
$$
\begin{aligned}
\alpha^2
&=\left|\gamma_u^2\mathbf a+\frac{\gamma_u^4q\mathbf u}{c^2}\right|^2
-\frac{\gamma_u^8q^2}{c^2}\\
&=\gamma_u^4a^2+\frac{2\gamma_u^6q^2}{c^2}
+\frac{\gamma_u^8q^2}{c^2}\left(\frac{u^2}{c^2}-1\right)\\
&=\boxed{\gamma_u^4\mathbf a\cdot\mathbf a+\frac{\gamma_u^6(\mathbf u\cdot\mathbf a)^2}{c^2}}.
\end{aligned}
$$
The last step uses $1-u^2/c^2=\gamma_u^{-2}$. This <proper acceleration from coordinate velocity and acceleration> also reads $\alpha^2=\gamma_u^6a_\parallel^2+\gamma_u^4a_\perp^2$, providing the familiar one-dimensional limit $\alpha=\gamma_u^3|a_\parallel|$. Each expression has units $\mathrm{m^2\,s^{-4}}$.

= b
{parent=B2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Write $\Gamma_a(v)=(1-v^2/a^2)^{-1/2}$. The first proposed <Lorentz transformation> is
$$
x'=\Gamma_a(v)(x-vt),\qquad
t'=\Gamma_a(v)\left(t-\frac{vx}{a^2}\right).
$$
For the next transformation, write $\Gamma_b(u)=(1-u^2/b^2)^{-1/2}$ and substitute the first pair into $x''=\Gamma_b(u)(x'-ut')$ and $t''=\Gamma_b(u)(t'-ux'/b^2)$. The result is
$$
\begin{aligned}
x''&=\Gamma_a(v)\Gamma_b(u)
\left[\left(1+\frac{uv}{a^2}\right)x-(u+v)t\right],\\
t''&=\Gamma_a(v)\Gamma_b(u)
\left[\left(1+\frac{uv}{b^2}\right)t
-\left(\frac{v}{a^2}+\frac{u}{b^2}\right)x\right].
\end{aligned}
$$
The direct transformation between $S$ and $S''$ must belong to the same standard boost family with the same calibration of space and time units. Such a transformation has one common factor multiplying the coefficient of $x$ in $x''$ and that of $t$ in $t''$. Equality of these coefficients for generic $uv\ne0$ requires
$$
1+\frac{uv}{a^2}=1+\frac{uv}{b^2},\qquad
\boxed{a^2=b^2.}
$$
The case $uv=0$ alone cannot constrain the scales, but a transformation law must work for arbitrary successive boosts. Once the scales agree, their composed <velocity> is $V=(u+v)/(1+uv/a^2)$ and the common factor is $\Gamma_a(V)$. This establishes the <universality of an invariant speed under boost composition> within the proposed family.

**Finiteness needs physical input.** Sending $a^2$ to infinity gives the consistent <Galilean transformation> $x'=x-vt$, $t'=t$. Its boosts compose with $V=u+v$ and satisfy a relativity principle. Thus the composition calculation itself does *not* prove that $a^2$ cannot be infinite. The finite propagation speed of light in vacuum and its invariance, supplied by the second of the <postulates of special relativity>, rule out this limit for physical spacetime. Under a <Galilean transformation>, a ray with laboratory speed $c$ would have speed $c-v$ in the moving frame, in conflict with that postulate.

Finally, the common invariant scale must be identified with the physical <speed of light>, rather than an arbitrary speed measured in one frame. Requiring a ray $x=ct$ also to obey $x'=ct'$ gives
$$
\frac{c-v}{1-vc/a^2}=c
\quad\Longrightarrow\quad a^2=c^2\qquad(v\ne0).
$$
Hence **the physical scale is $a=c$**, taking the positive speed. The postulate about light identifies and requires a finite invariant scale; closure alone only establishes its universality. This distinction is necessary for a logically valid answer to the question's request to exclude infinity.

= B3
{c}
{parent=Section B}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=B3}

Use the $+---$ <metric signature> and abbreviate $f(r)=1-2\mu/r$, with $\mu=GM/c^2$ a length. Dots initially mean differentiation with respect to an <affine parameter> $\lambda$. The <geodesic Lagrangian> in the equatorial plane is
$$
\mathcal L=\frac12\left(c^2f\dot t^2-\frac{\dot r^2}{f}-r^2\dot\phi^2\right).
$$
The <planarity of geodesics in spherical symmetry> permits this plane to be chosen. More explicitly, the full angular <Euler-Lagrange equation> is $\ddot\theta+2\dot r\dot\theta/r-\sin\theta\cos\theta\,\dot\phi^2=0$, so initial data $\theta=\pi/2$, $\dot\theta=0$ preserve it.

Both $t$ and $\phi$ are <cyclic coordinates>. Their <Euler-Lagrange equations> give conserved conjugate momenta $p_t=c^2f\dot t$ and $p_\phi=-r^2\dot\phi$. Defining constants $k,h$ therefore gives
$$
\boxed{f\dot t=k,\qquad r^2\dot\phi=h.}
$$
The corresponding second-order <geodesic equations> are
$$
\ddot t+\frac{f'}f\dot r\dot t=0,\qquad
\ddot\phi+\frac2r\dot r\dot\phi=0,
$$
and variation of $r$ gives
$$
\boxed{\ddot r-\frac{f'}{2f}\dot r^2+\frac{c^2ff'}2\dot t^2-fr\dot\phi^2=0,\qquad f'=\frac{2\mu}{r^2}.}
$$
The <geodesic Lagrangian> has no explicit $\lambda$ dependence. Its conserved Lagrangian <energy function> is $\dot x^\alpha\partial\mathcal L/\partial\dot x^\alpha-\mathcal L=\mathcal L$, since it is quadratic in the tangent components. Thus twice this function is a constant:
$$
\boxed{c^2f\dot t^2-\frac{\dot r^2}{f}-r^2\dot\phi^2=\kappa.}
$$
This is the constant squared tangent norm of an affinely parametrized <geodesic>.

For a massive particle choose $\lambda=\tau$, its <proper time>, and obtain **$\kappa=c^2$**. For a <photon>, the tangent is null and **$\kappa=0$**. A general rescaling of a massive particle's <affine parameter> rescales a positive $\kappa$; the numerical value $c^2$ requires the <proper time> choice. With that choice, $h=L_z/m$ is signed <specific angular momentum>, of units $\mathrm{m^2\,s^{-1}}$, and $k=E/(mc^2)$ is dimensionless conserved specific <Killing energy>. The time coordinate is normalized to <proper time> at infinity, so this $E$ is the particle's conserved <energy> referred to infinity. A <photon>'s constants scale with the normalization of its null tangent; it has no <rest frame> or <proper time> parametrization.

Substitution of $\dot t=k/f$ and $\dot\phi=h/r^2$ into the norm gives the general radial first integral
$$
\dot r^2=c^2k^2-f\left(\kappa+\frac{h^2}{r^2}\right).
$$
For a massive particle this becomes
$$
\boxed{c^2(k^2-1)=\dot r^2-\frac{2\mu c^2}{r}
+\frac{h^2}{r^2}\left(1-\frac{2\mu}{r}\right).}
$$
Adopting the usual half-normalization for the <effective potential for timelike Schwarzschild geodesics>, define
$$
\frac12\dot r^2+V_{\rm eff}(r)=\frac{c^2}2(k^2-1),\qquad
\boxed{V_{\rm eff}(r)=-\frac{\mu c^2}{r}+\frac{h^2}{2r^2}-\frac{\mu h^2}{r^3}.}
$$
All terms have units $\mathrm{m^2\,s^{-2}}$, or <energy> per unit <mass>. The first two terms are the Newtonian gravitational and centrifugal contributions; the last is the relativistic correction.

A <circular orbit> requires both $\dot r=0$ and $V'_{\rm eff}(R)=0$. Differentiating at fixed $h$ gives
$$
0=\frac{\mu c^2}{R^2}-\frac{h^2}{R^3}+\frac{3\mu h^2}{R^4},
\qquad
\boxed{h^2=\frac{\mu c^2R^2}{R-3\mu}},\qquad R>3\mu.
$$
The radial first integral also gives
$$
k^2=\frac{(R-2\mu)^2}{R(R-3\mu)}.
$$
For the future-directed circular orbit at $R=4\mu$, these constants are $|h|=4\mu c$, $k=1$, and $f=1/2$. Therefore
$$
\dot t=2,\qquad |\dot\phi|=\frac{c}{4\mu},\qquad
\left|\frac{d\phi}{dt}\right|=\frac{c}{8\mu}.
$$
Since <Schwarzschild time> agrees with the clock time of a static observer at infinity, the intended infinity-referred tangential <orbital speed> is
$$
\boxed{v_\infty=R\left|\frac{d\phi}{dt}\right|=\frac c2.}
$$
This uses the circumferential <areal radius> $R$ with the clock at infinity; it is not a local measurement made at the orbit. A local static observer's clock instead has $d\tau_{\rm stat}=\sqrt f\,dt$ and measures $v_{\rm local}=v_\infty/\sqrt f=\boxed{c/\sqrt2}$. These two operationally different speeds explain the significance of the phrase “at infinity”.

The <effective-potential stability of a circular orbit> follows from
$$
V''_{\rm eff}(R)=\frac{\mu c^2(R-6\mu)}{R^3(R-3\mu)}.
$$
Consequently the $R=4\mu$ orbit exists but is unstable to radial perturbations. The original plot below shows its position at a maximum, for $h=4\mu c$.

\Image[2018-effective-potential.png]
{title=Schwarzschild effective potential for specific angular momentum h = 4 mu c}

For a weak-field <elliptic orbit>, the relativistic correction makes the direction of closest approach advance after each radial cycle. The derivations under i and ii below give
$$
\boxed{\Delta\phi=\frac{6\pi GM}{c^2a_{\rm orb}(1-e^2)}}
$$
per orbit to first order, where $a_{\rm orb}$ is the <semi-major axis> and $e$ the <orbital eccentricity>. For <Mercury>, the additional <Schwarzschild perihelion precession> is approximately **43 arcseconds per century**, over and above the much larger Newtonian precession produced primarily by other planets. It is the unexplained residual after those effects have been included that provides the classic quantitative test of <general relativity>; one should not identify the whole observed precession with this relativistic correction.https://ntrs.nasa.gov/api/citations/20180000952/downloads/20180000952.pdf[PDF p. 2: planetary perturbations about 531.63 arcseconds per Julian century and additional relativistic precession about 42.98 arcseconds per Julian century]{ref}

The labels i and ii in the original paper introduce optional orbit-equation aids, rather than separate mandatory subquestions. They are retained here for derivations of both aids and of the perihelion result.

= i
{parent=B3}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

The relativistic orbit-shape equation can be derived without dividing by $\dot r$ at a turning point. Substitute the norm constraint into the radial <geodesic equation> to eliminate $\dot t$ and $\dot r^2$. Using $f'=2\mu/r^2$ gives
$$
\ddot r=-\frac{\mu\kappa}{r^2}+\frac{h^2}{r^3}-\frac{3\mu h^2}{r^4}.
$$
For a massive particle with <proper time> as parameter, $\kappa=c^2$. Put $u=1/r$, and use primes in this paragraph for <derivatives> with respect to $\phi$. The constant <specific angular momentum> gives $\dot\phi=hu^2$, so the <chain rule> yields
$$
\dot r=\frac{dr}{d\phi}\dot\phi=-hu',\qquad
\ddot r=-h^2u^2u''.
$$
Substituting these expressions into the radial equation and dividing by $h^2u^2$ for a nonradial orbit gives
$$
\boxed{u''+u-3\mu u^2=\frac{\mu c^2}{h^2}=\frac{GM}{h^2}.}
$$
The extra $3\mu u^2$ term is responsible for <Schwarzschild perihelion precession>. It vanishes in the Newtonian weak-field limit $\mu/r\to0$. The derivation uses a massive particle; for a <null geodesic>, $\kappa=0$ and the constant $GM/h^2$ on the right is absent.

= ii
{parent=B3}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

In <Newtonian gravity>, the radial equation with conserved <specific angular momentum> is $\ddot r=-GM/r^2+h^2/r^3$. The substitution $u=1/r$ therefore gives the linear <differential equation>
$$
u''+u=\frac{GM}{h^2}.
$$
Choosing $\phi=0$ along perihelion, its <elliptic orbit> solution is
$$
\boxed{u_0(\phi)=\frac{1+e\cos\phi}{p},\qquad
p=\frac{h^2}{GM}=a_{\rm orb}(1-e^2).}
$$
Here $p$ is the <semilatus rectum>. One can check the last equality directly from $r_{\rm peri}=p/(1+e)$, $r_{\rm apo}=p/(1-e)$, and $a_{\rm orb}=(r_{\rm peri}+r_{\rm apo})/2$.

To find <Schwarzschild perihelion precession>, write $u=u_0+\delta u$ in the relativistic equation and retain first order in $\mu/p$. The <linear ordinary differential equation> for the correction is
$$
\delta u''+\delta u=3\mu u_0^2
=\frac{3\mu}{p^2}\left(1+\frac{e^2}{2}+2e\cos\phi+\frac{e^2}{2}\cos2\phi\right).
$$
The constant and double-frequency forcing change the orbit's shape without accumulating an angular shift. The resonant $\cos\phi$ term produces
$$
\delta u_{\rm sec}=\frac{3\mu e}{p^2}\,\phi\sin\phi,
$$
since $(d^2/d\phi^2+1)(\phi\sin\phi)=2\cos\phi$. The oscillating part of the perturbed orbit is consequently the first-order expansion of
$$
\frac e p\cos\big[(1-3\mu/p)\phi\big].
$$
Successive perihelia are separated by $2\pi/(1-3\mu/p)$, slightly more than a full revolution. Their forward angular advance is thus
$$
\boxed{\Delta\phi=\frac{6\pi\mu}{p}
=\frac{6\pi GM}{c^2a_{\rm orb}(1-e^2)}
+O\!\left(\frac{\mu^2}{p^2}\right)}
$$
in radians per orbit. This leading expression applies when $\mu/p\ll1$. For a perfectly <circular orbit> there is no uniquely located perihelion; the same expression describes the limiting mismatch of radial and azimuthal frequencies for a small nonzero <orbital eccentricity>.

= B4
{c}
{parent=Section B}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=B4}

**Local equivalence and tides.** In <Newtonian mechanics>, <inertial mass> $m_{\rm i}$ measures the response to a <force>, whereas passive <gravitational mass> $m_{\rm g}$ couples to an imposed <Newtonian gravitational field>. A freely falling body obeys
$$
m_{\rm i}\mathbf a=m_{\rm g}\mathbf g,\qquad
\mathbf a=\frac{m_{\rm g}}{m_{\rm i}}\mathbf g.
$$
The observed composition independence of <free fall> says that this ratio is universal. With a common choice of units it is one. This is the Newtonian content of the <weak equivalence principle> for <test particles>; it does not by itself specify all nongravitational physics.

The <Einstein equivalence principle> further says that local nongravitational experiments in a sufficiently small freely falling laboratory have the form predicted by <special relativity>, independently of the laboratory's location and motion. In such a <local inertial frame>, the <metric tensor> is locally the <Minkowski metric> and the <Christoffel symbols> vanish at the selected event. The <strong equivalence principle> extends the universality claim to local gravitational experiments and bodies with significant gravitational self-energy. None of these statements removes measurable <tidal forces> across a finite apparatus.

In an ideal small freely falling lift, a released ball initially floats relative to the cabin, because both undergo the same <free fall>. Two separated balls, however, need not retain their separation: they sample different parts of the <gravitational field>. For a point source, linearizing $\mathbf g=-GM\mathbf r/r^3$ across a small separation $\boldsymbol\xi$ gives the <tidal tensor> prediction
$$
\boxed{\frac{d^2\boldsymbol\xi}{dt^2}
=\frac{GM}{r^3}\big[3\widehat{\mathbf r}(\widehat{\mathbf r}\cdot\boldsymbol\xi)-\boldsymbol\xi\big].}
$$
A radial separation stretches with coefficient $2GM/r^3$, whereas a transverse separation is compressed with coefficient $-GM/r^3$. Initially parallel <velocities> therefore develop relative motion even though the individual bodies experience zero <proper acceleration>.

The relativistic statement is <geodesic deviation>. With the <Riemann curvature tensor> convention
$$
R^\mu{}_{\nu\rho\sigma}
=\partial_\rho\Gamma^\mu{}_{\sigma\nu}-\partial_\sigma\Gamma^\mu{}_{\rho\nu}
+\Gamma^\mu{}_{\rho\alpha}\Gamma^\alpha{}_{\sigma\nu}
-\Gamma^\mu{}_{\sigma\alpha}\Gamma^\alpha{}_{\rho\nu},
$$
a connecting vector between neighboring freely falling <worldlines> satisfies
$$
\boxed{\frac{D^2\xi^\mu}{d\tau^2}
=-R^\mu{}_{\nu\rho\sigma}U^\nu\xi^\rho U^\sigma.}
$$
Here $D/d\tau$ is the <covariant derivative> along the reference <geodesic>, and $U^\mu$ its <four-velocity>. <Curvature> can be nonzero where the <Christoffel symbols> have been made zero. In the weak, static, slow-motion limit this equation reduces to $\ddot\xi^i=-\partial_i\partial_j\Phi\,\xi^j$, recovering the <tidal tensor> above. A finite falling lift can detect <gravity> through these relative <accelerations>; local equivalence does not imply a globally uniform field.

**Weak-field connection and potential.** Take $x^0=ct$ and the $+---$ <metric signature>. There is an index error in the printed formula for the <Christoffel symbols>: its first differentiated metric component must be $g_{\nu0}$, not $g_{\mu0}$. The correctly contracted expression is
$$
\Gamma^\mu{}_{00}=\frac12g^{\mu\nu}
\left(2\partial_0g_{\nu0}-\partial_\nu g_{00}\right).
$$
The offending term vanishes under the stipulated time independence, so this correction does not change the intended calculation. Write $g_{\mu\nu}=\eta_{\mu\nu}+h_{\mu\nu}$ and treat both $h$ and its <derivatives> as first order. To that order $g^{00}=1-h_{00}$, $g^{0i}=O(h)$, and $g^{ij}=-\delta^{ij}+O(h)$. A stationary <metric tensor> gives
$$
\Gamma^\mu{}_{00}=-\frac12g^{\mu j}\partial_jh_{00}.
$$
For the temporal component the inverse-metric coefficient is already first order, so
$$
\boxed{\Gamma^0{}_{00}=O(h\,\partial h)\approx0.}
$$
For a spatial component the zeroth-order inverse metric contributes:
$$
\boxed{\Gamma^i{}_{00}\approx\frac12\delta^{ij}\partial_jh_{00}
=\frac12\frac{\partial h_{00}}{\partial x^i}.}
$$
The time component of the approximate <geodesic equation> says that $dt/d\tau$ is constant to the retained order. In its spatial component, convert the <proper time> derivatives to coordinate-time derivatives and cancel $(dt/d\tau)^2$:
$$
\frac{d^2x^i}{dt^2}\approx-c^2\Gamma^i{}_{00}
\approx-\frac{c^2}{2}\partial_i h_{00}.
$$
Comparison with the Newtonian equation $d^2\mathbf x/dt^2=-\boldsymbol\nabla\Phi$ identifies $h_{00}=2\Phi/c^2+C$. For an isolated source, choose the <gravitational potential> to tend to zero at infinity and normalize the time coordinate there, giving $C=0$. Thus
$$
\boxed{g_{00}\approx1+\frac{2\Phi}{c^2}.}
$$
The quantity $\Phi$ is the Newtonian <gravitational potential>, or <gravitational potential energy> per unit <mass>, in $\mathrm{J\,kg^{-1}}=\mathrm{m^2\,s^{-2}}$. For a point source, $\Phi=-GM/r$. A static clock has $d\tau=\sqrt{g_{00}}\,dt\approx(1+\Phi/c^2)dt$, connecting the <Newtonian limit> with <gravitational time dilation>.

**Frequency of the orbiting source.** Treat the gas blob as a <test particle> on a <timelike circular Schwarzschild geodesic>, with pressure and other nongravitational forces negligible. Treat our very distant location as infinity, so the observer's own <gravitational potential> is negligible. These are the assumptions that make a unique answer in terms of $R_S/r$ possible.

Put $f=1-R_S/r$ and use a local static observer at the emission point as an intermediate measuring frame. The remote observer lies on the symmetry axis of the orbit. A ray arriving on that axis has zero conserved axial <angular momentum>, so its local direction is perpendicular to the emitter's azimuthal <velocity>. Bending within a meridional plane does not alter this orthogonality. The transverse <relativistic Doppler effect> therefore gives
$$
\nu_{\rm stat}=\frac{\nu_{\rm em}}{\gamma_v},\qquad
\gamma_v=\frac1{\sqrt{1-v^2/c^2}},
$$
where $v$ is the orbital <speed> measured by that local static observer. The <gravitational redshift between static Schwarzschild observers> then gives
$$
\frac{\nu_{\rm ob}}{\nu_{\rm em}}=\frac{\sqrt{1-R_S/r}}{\gamma_v}.
$$
A gravitational <circular orbit> has $v^2=GM/r+O((GM/r)^2/c^2)$ in the weak-field limit, so $v^2/c^2=R_S/(2r)+O((R_S/r)^2)$. Expanding both factors,
$$
\frac{\nu_{\rm ob}}{\nu_{\rm em}}
=\left(1-\frac{R_S}{2r}\right)
\left(1-\frac{R_S}{4r}\right)+O\!\left(\frac{R_S^2}{r^2}\right)
=1-\frac{3R_S}{4r}+O\!\left(\frac{R_S^2}{r^2}\right).
$$
Solving for the emitted <frequency> gives
$$
\boxed{\nu_{\rm em}=\nu_{\rm ob}\left[1+\frac{3R_S}{4r}+O\!\left(\frac{R_S^2}{r^2}\right)\right].}
$$
The gravitational part is $R_S/(2r)$ and the transverse kinematic <time dilation> contributes another $R_S/(4r)$; the absence of line-of-sight motion does not remove this second effect.

As an exact check, the local speed on a <timelike circular Schwarzschild geodesic> satisfies $v^2/c^2=\mu/(r-2\mu)$, where $\mu=GM/c^2=R_S/2$. Therefore the <face-on frequency shift of a circular Schwarzschild source> is
$$
\frac{\nu_{\rm ob}}{\nu_{\rm em}}=\sqrt{1-\frac{3\mu}{r}}
=\sqrt{1-\frac{3R_S}{2r}},
$$
whose expansion agrees with the boxed answer. If the gas is supported or accelerated by pressure or magnetic stresses rather than following a <geodesic>, its <orbital speed> is not fixed by the mass and radius alone; the appropriate first-order result is instead $\nu_{\rm em}\approx\nu_{\rm ob}[1+GM/(rc^2)+v^2/(2c^2)]$, with $v$ supplied independently.
